{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense fails because it conflates the logical justification for 'preference' with the strategic utility of 'selection.' Popper explicitly acknowledges that scientists prefer better-corroborated theories to act on them (e.g., building bridges), which implies a rational expectation of future success. By redefining this preference solely as a 'methodological rule to maximize severity,' the target sidesteps the core dilemma: acting on a theory without believing it is more likely to be true (or at least not false) is pragmatically incoherent. If the preference is not based on an inductive hope that past survival indicates future reliability, then the 'preference' is merely arbitrary from the perspective of action, rendering the defense circular or empty regarding the actual practice of science it claims to justify.","premise":"Acting on a theory (e.g., building a bridge) inherently requires a belief in its reliability or probability of success, distinct from merely selecting it for further testing.","successor_question":"Can a purely methodological rule for test selection ever suffice as a rational basis for high-stakes practical action without implicitly relying on inductive assumptions about future performance?","target":"d51779d908becef983c5153766937e4b0bf74f9e63099ab47877563cc0f6b96d"}]}